-- title:
--   §5, pp.11–12 — The normal form: proof of (C3)⇒(C2)
-- statement:
--   Let $f:\Sigma^*\to\mathbb Q$ be an invertible rational series on a finite alphabet, with minimal representation $f(w)=\lambda M_w\gamma$ ($M_a$ invertible $d\times d$ rational matrices), $\Gamma_f\le GL_d(\mathbb Q)$ the group generated by the $M_a$, $\mathcal G_f\subseteq GL_d(\overline{\mathbb Q})$ its Zariski closure and $\mathcal G_f^\circ$ its identity component.
--
--   If $\mathcal G_f^\circ$ is triangularizable over $\mathbb Q$ and any two unipotent elements of $\mathcal G_f$ commute (C3), then $f$ is computed by a CCRA whose control is a permutation automaton, in which every register is updated in isolation by a map $x\mapsto\alpha x+\beta$ with $\alpha\in\mathbb Q^\times$, $\beta\in\mathbb Q$, and whose outputs are linear combinations of products of distinct registers (C2).
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: condition on "the" minimal representation of $f$ → hypotheses: $(\lambda,M,\gamma)$ is any minimal representation of $f$ with invertible letter matrices; (C3), (C4) are stated for its $M$; reason: minimal representations are unique up to simultaneous conjugation and (C3), (C4) are conjugation invariant (p.2); same instances
--   - encoding: copyless polynomial expressions; (C2)'s isolated updates and output shape → syntax trees with an occurrence multiset (copyless = no repeated occurrence across the tuple); (C2)'s update and output conditions stated on the functions the expressions compute; reason: copylessness is a property of the written expression; a function of the form $\alpha x_i+\beta$ is computed by the literal expression
--   - encoding: Zariski closure via polynomials in the entries and $\det^{-1}$ → polynomials in the entries, closed sets taken relative to $GL_d(\overline{\mathbb Q})$; reason: multiplying by a power of $\det$, a unit on $GL$, gives the same closed subsets of $GL$
--   - encoding: identity component $\mathcal G^\circ$ of the algebraic group → the union of all Zariski-connected subsets of $\mathcal G$ containing $1$ (the connected component of $1$); reason: this is the identity component of a linear algebraic group; its group and finite-index properties (A1) are theorems, not definitions
-- source:
--   Zeng–Cao–Dai–Wang–Ding, Copyless Cost Register Automata and Algebraic Groups, STOC 2027 submission (manuscript sha256 434d3094df2665371c402d5b5291b3c7f3a27a0d79384e4a8ec3ca3a46a92543), §5, pp.11–12 (PDF pp.13–14), "This proves (C3)⇒(C2)"

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_CopylessCRA_CCRA
import Definitions.Def_CopylessCRA_IsRep
import Definitions.Def_CopylessCRA_zariskiClosure

namespace CopylessCRA

/-- **The normal form: proof of (C3)⇒(C2)** (§5). For an invertible rational series `f` with minimal
representation `(λ, (M_a), γ)`: if `𝒢°_f` is triangularizable over `ℚ` and any two unipotent elements
of `𝒢_f` commute, then `f` is computed by a CCRA whose control is a permutation automaton, whose
registers are updated in isolation by maps `x ↦ αx + β` (`α ∈ ℚ×`, `β ∈ ℚ`), and whose outputs are
linear combinations of products of distinct registers.

Zeng–Cao–Dai–Wang–Ding, *Copyless Cost Register Automata and Algebraic Groups*, STOC 2027
submission (manuscript sha256 434d3094…), §5, pp.11–12 (PDF pp.13–14), "This proves (C3)⇒(C2)".

**Formalization Note** This is the implication (C3)⇒(C2) of `theorem_1`, with the same encoding. -/
theorem c3_imp_c2 {α : Type*} [Fintype α] (f : List α → ℚ) {d : ℕ} (lam : Fin d → ℚ)
    (M : α → Matrix (Fin d) (Fin d) ℚ) (γ : Fin d → ℚ) (hrep : IsRep f lam M γ)
    (hmin : IsMinimalRep lam M γ) (hM : ∀ a, IsUnit (M a)) :
    C3 M → C2 f := by sorry

end CopylessCRA
